references/RULES.md:22: The host selects Normal or Quick mode. Players join private rooms by room code. The host is implicitly ready; every other player must explicitly become ready before the host can start. Changing mode clears every non-host ready state. Public matchmaking creates two-player matches in either mode. Spectators and chat remain outside the first online release.
references/RULES.md:30: A guest may register an account using a private login name and password. Registration upgrades the existing identity and preserves room membership, replay access, and history. During registration the player may choose one reviewed prefix and one reviewed noun exactly once. Login names are private and never appear to other players. Account sessions roll for 30 days. The first release has no email collection or password recovery.
references/RULES.md:32: ## 1.2 Public Matchmaking And Ladder
references/RULES.md:34: Normal and Quick each have separate public queues for 4-player, 8-player, and 12-player matches and separate account ratings. Guests may enter the same queues but every match containing a guest is unranked. A match affects rating only when both players are registered accounts.
references/RULES.md:36: Matching begins within 100 rating points, expands by 50 points every 10 seconds, and stops expanding at 500 points. A proposed match requires both players to accept within 15 seconds. Players may cancel or decline without penalty. A disconnect during confirmation does not immediately reject the proposal; the player may reconnect and accept before the absolute deadline. If only one player accepts, that player returns to the queue with the original enqueue time and expanded rating range.
references/RULES.md:38: After every player in a proposed 4-player, 8-player, or 12-player group accepts, the server creates a hidden, locked room at that final size and immediately starts the selected mode. Matchmade rooms expose no join code, accept no additional players, and do not permit kicking or mode changes.
references/RULES.md:42: Candidate groups are built in queue order. Every added candidate creates a new group average rating, and every member's difference from that average must fit that member's current time-expanded rating range. Confirmation shows final player count and whether the match is ranked, but not names, tiers, or ratings.
references/RULES.md:44: If confirmation fails, players who accepted remain grouped with their original enqueue times and receive priority replacement players. A cancelling player alone leaves the retained group. After replacements fill all vacancies, every member receives a fresh 15-second confirmation and must accept again. Once started, every ranked match is fully rated. Active leave and 30-second disconnect elimination count as ordinary losses.
references/RULES.md:46: An identity has at most one global matchmaking state across devices and tabs. A player in a private room cannot queue. In an unstarted private room, a non-host may leave and immediately rejoin. If the host leaves, ownership transfers to the earliest-joined remaining member; an empty room is deleted.
references/RULES.md:48: In a pure-account multiplayer match, final placement is reverse elimination order with the winner first. Players eliminated in the same resolution tie. Each player's Glicko-2 update compares that player against every opponent: a higher placement scores 1, a lower placement scores 0, and a tie scores 0.5. Active leave and disconnect elimination use their actual elimination position. A fallback match remains ranked when all final participants are accounts.
references/RULES.md:50: Account ratings use Glicko-2 with initial rating 1500, rating deviation 350, volatility 0.06, tau 0.5, and immediate per-match updates. Normal and Quick ratings are independent. The first five completed ranked matches are provisional and excluded from the Top 10 leaderboard.
references/RULES.md:52: Rating tiers are Bronze below 1200, Silver 1200-1399, Gold 1400-1599, Platinum 1600-1799, Diamond 1800-1999, and Champion at 2000 or above.
references/RULES.md:144: - one physical ordinary card ranked 2 through 10.
references/RULES.md:216: When a character reaches zero HP from ordinary attack damage or a turn-timeout penalty, the room pauses in `WAITING_FOR_JOKER`.
references/RULES.md:314: The room is paused during rescue and replacement selection. Only the player currently offered rescue or replacement may submit the corresponding command. When rescue or replacement finishes, the original attacker's turn is complete and play continues from the next surviving seat after that attacker.
references/RULES.md:321: - Reconnection with the same signed guest identity restores that player's room projection.
references/RULES.md:323: - Turn and disconnect deadlines run independently. If a disconnected player's normal turn expires first, apply the standard 5 HP and random-discard penalty. Do not pause the room solely for disconnection.
references/RULES.md:341: Completed full replays are available only to match participants identified by their signed guest identity. They may reveal all hands, reserve characters, deck order, seed, and random outcomes after the match. Full command replays are retained for 14 days; anonymized result summaries may be retained longer.
AGENTS.md:37: - Room-code flow; host chooses mode; all players must be ready.
AGENTS.md:38: - Automatic guest identity is the default. Durable accounts and two-player public matchmaking are in scope; spectators and chat remain deferred.
AGENTS.md:40: - Active room state is stored in Redis; completed match summaries are stored in PostgreSQL.
AGENTS.md:46: - `apps/server`: HTTP/Socket.IO transport, identity, rooms, timers, persistence, and projections.
AGENTS.md:68: - Every room state has a monotonic `version`. Client patches include base and next versions; mismatch triggers a full snapshot request.
AGENTS.md:69: - Private state is projected per viewer. Never broadcast the canonical room state or another player's hand/reserve characters.
